    <div id="about-body" tabindex="-1">

      <div class="about-section">
        <h3>&#x2139;&#xFE0E; Description</h3>
        <p>Self-hosted, client-side encrypted password manager served by a <strong>PHP-capable web server</strong> — or, with no PHP at all, by the bundled standalone <strong>Node.js server</strong> (<code>server.js</code>). All encryption and decryption happens entirely in your browser using the <strong>WebCrypto API</strong> — the server only stores and serves ciphertext and never sees your plaintext passwords.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:4px">Open source, released under the <a href="https://opensource.org/license/mit" target="_blank" rel="noopener" data-tip="Open in New Window/Tab: https://opensource.org/license/mit">MIT License</a>.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:4px; color:var(--text-2); font-size:.85em;">Version <a id="about-version" href="https://github.com/nemesis2/PasswordVault" target="_blank" rel="noopener" data-tip="Open in New Window/Tab: https://github.com/nemesis2/PasswordVault">1.1.11</a></p>
      </div>

      <div class="about-section" id="about-vault-tools">
        <h3>&#x1F6E0;&#xFE0E; Vault Tools</h3>
        <p><strong>Audit</strong> decrypts every entry locally and flags reused, weak, fair, empty, or stale (not changed in over a year) passwords — names only are shown and nothing leaves this device; secure notes have no password and are skipped. <strong>Passkeys</strong> lists which entries store a WebAuthn passkey, by name and site — this app can only view and delete a stored passkey; the companion browser extension is what creates and signs one. <strong>Trash</strong> lists entries you have deleted so you can restore them or remove them permanently; deletions are kept server-side for up to 30 days (capped at the 100 most recent; ciphertext only). <strong>Change Passwords</strong> re-encrypts the whole vault under new master passwords in one atomic save. <strong>Sign</strong> stores a keyed signature (HMAC under your master passwords) over the whole vault; it is checked automatically on every unlock and refreshed after every save, so tampering, corruption, or a rolled-back copy is detected. Use the button only to accept a deliberate manual edit or backup restore as the new baseline.</p>
        <div class="vt-btns">
          <button class="btn btn-ghost" data-action="audit-vault" data-tip="Find reused / weak / stale passwords (local only)">&#x1F50E;&#xFE0E; Audit</button>
          <button class="btn btn-ghost" data-action="list-passkeys" data-tip="List entries that store a passkey (local only)">&#x1F510;&#xFE0E; Passkeys</button>
          <button class="btn btn-ghost" data-action="open-trash" data-tip="View and restore deleted entries">&#x1F5D1;&#xFE0E; Trash</button>
          <button class="btn btn-ghost" data-action="toggle-chpw" data-tip="Re-encrypt the vault under new passwords">&#x1F511;&#xFE0E; Change Passwords</button>
          <button class="btn btn-ghost" data-action="resign-vault" data-tip="Sign the current contents as the new integrity baseline">&#x270D;&#xFE0E; Sign</button>
        </div>
        <div id="integrity-status" style="display:none"></div>
        <div id="audit-locked" class="vt-locked-note" style="display:none">Enter both passwords and unlock the vault first.</div>
        <div id="audit-result" style="display:none"></div>
