      <div class="about-section" id="about-backup">
        <h3>&#x1F4E6;&#xFE0E; Backup &amp; Migration</h3>
        <p><strong>Export</strong> downloads the encrypted database exactly as stored on the server (ciphertext only). <strong>Import</strong> restores an exported <code>.lines</code> file, replacing the whole vault in one atomic save — every imported entry is decrypted with the passwords in the key fields first, so an import that does not match those passwords is refused and nothing is changed (the server also keeps a backup of the current vault before importing). <strong>Export CSV</strong> writes every entry to a <em>plaintext</em> file (passwords, TOTP secrets, and notes in the clear, with none of the vault&rsquo;s protection) for migrating into another password manager &mdash; it warns and asks for confirmation first, and you should delete the file as soon as you are done. <strong>Import CSV</strong> reads a CSV exported by another manager (Bitwarden, Chrome, 1Password, &hellip;) and <em>adds</em> those entries to your vault, re-encrypting each one with the passwords in the key fields (your existing entries are kept). <strong>Import KeePass</strong> reads a KeePass 2 XML export (File &rarr; Export &rarr; KeePass XML in KeePass/KeePassXC) and adds those entries the same way. <strong>Import 1Password</strong> reads a 1Password <code>.1pux</code> export and does the same &mdash; the encrypted <code>.kdbx</code> file itself isn&rsquo;t supported, so export to XML/CSV first.</p>
        <div class="vt-btns">
          <button class="btn btn-ghost" data-action="export-vault" data-tip="Download the encrypted database">&#x2B07;&#xFE0E; Export</button>
          <button class="btn btn-ghost" data-action="import-vault" data-tip="Replace the whole vault from an exported .lines file">&#x2B06;&#xFE0E; Import</button>
          <button class="btn btn-ghost" data-action="export-csv" data-tip="Export all entries as a PLAINTEXT CSV (no encryption)">&#x2B07;&#xFE0E; Export CSV</button>
          <button class="btn btn-ghost" data-action="import-csv" data-tip="Add entries from a CSV exported by another password manager">&#x2B06;&#xFE0E; Import CSV</button>
          <button class="btn btn-ghost" data-action="import-keepass" data-tip="Add entries from a KeePass 2 XML export (File &rarr; Export &rarr; KeePass XML in KeePass/KeePassXC)">&#x2B06;&#xFE0E; Import KeePass</button>
          <button class="btn btn-ghost" data-action="import-1pux" data-tip="Add entries from a 1Password .1pux export">&#x2B06;&#xFE0E; Import 1Password</button>
        </div>
        <div id="import-status" style="display:none"></div>
      </div>

      <div class="about-section" id="about-kdf">
        <h3>&#x2699;&#xFE0E; Change KDF Parameters</h3>
        <p>Tunes the Argon2id key-derivation cost for the <strong>whole vault</strong>. Higher values make every unlock slower for you but brute-forcing far slower for an attacker. The change re-encrypts every entry in your browser under the same passwords (taken from the key fields above), then saves in a single atomic replace — if anything fails, the vault is left untouched. Memory must be 64&ndash;1024 MiB, iterations 2&ndash;10; parallelism is fixed at 1.</p>

        <div class="vt-btns">
          <button class="btn btn-ghost" data-action="toggle-kdf" data-tip="Re-encrypt the vault at a new Argon2id cost">&#x2699;&#xFE0E; Change KDF Parameters</button>
        </div>
        <div id="kdf-locked" class="vt-locked-note" style="display:none">Enter both passwords and unlock the vault first.</div>
        <div id="kdf-form" style="display:none;margin-top:10px">

          <p>Suggested settings by device class (the security column is a rough guide to how much memory-hardness an offline attacker faces):</p>
          <table class="kdf-table">
            <thead>
              <tr><th>Device class</th><th>Memory</th><th>Iter.</th><th>Security</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="kdf-class">Small portable (phone / low-RAM)</td><td class="kdf-num">64 MiB</td><td class="kdf-num">3</td><td class="kdf-sec-low">Baseline</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="kdf-class">Laptop / typical desktop</td><td class="kdf-num">128 MiB</td><td class="kdf-num">3</td><td class="kdf-sec-mid">Good (default)</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="kdf-class">Modern desktop (16 GB+)</td><td class="kdf-num">256 MiB</td><td class="kdf-num">4</td><td class="kdf-sec-high">Strong</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="kdf-class">Large workstation (32 GB+)</td><td class="kdf-num">512 MiB</td><td class="kdf-num">4</td><td class="kdf-sec-max">Very strong</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="kdf-class">Workstation, max</td><td class="kdf-num">1024 MiB</td><td class="kdf-num">5</td><td class="kdf-sec-max">Maximum</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
          <p class="chpw-note">A high memory setting multiplied by the parallel worker count must fit in this device's RAM — raise it gradually if unlocking starts to fail on low-memory devices.<br /></p>

          <div class="chpw-grid">
