      <div class="about-section" id="about-organizing">
        <h3>&#x1F3F7;&#xFE0E; Organizing &amp; Searching</h3>
        <p><strong>Tags.</strong> Each entry can carry a comma-separated list of tags (e.g. <code>work, email, personal</code>). Tags are normalized on save &mdash; lowercased, trimmed, and de-duplicated &mdash; and are stored <strong>inside the encrypted payload</strong>, exactly like the password and notes, so they are as protected as any other field and never appear in the database in the clear.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:6px"><strong>Command palette.</strong> Press <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>&#x202F;<kbd>K</kbd> (or <kbd>?</kbd>, or the &#x1F50D;&#xFE0E; toolbar button) to open the palette. Type to <strong>fuzzy-find</strong> entries by name, navigate the results with <kbd>&uarr;</kbd>&#x202F;<kbd>&darr;</kbd>, and press <kbd>Enter</kbd> to open the highlighted entry. With an entry highlighted you can act on it without leaving the keyboard: <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>&#x202F;<kbd>C</kbd> copies its password, <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>&#x202F;<kbd>U</kbd> copies its username, and <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>&#x202F;<kbd>E</kbd> opens it for editing (use <kbd>&#x2318;</kbd> in place of <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> on a Mac). The hint bar along the bottom of the palette is a reminder of these.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:6px"><strong>Command mode.</strong> Type a leading <code>&gt;</code> to switch the palette into command mode &mdash; a fuzzy-filtered list of vault actions (New entry, Lock, Export / Import, Audit, Passkeys, Trash, Change passwords / KDF, Toggle theme, and more). Actions that need the vault open are dimmed until both passwords are entered, and those that live in the About panel open it scrolled to the right section.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:6px"><strong>Scoped search.</strong> Within the palette, the leading character chooses what to match: type plainly to fuzzy-match entry <strong>names</strong>, prefix with <code>#</code> to search <strong>tags</strong> (e.g. <code>#work</code>), <code>@</code> to search <strong>notes</strong> (e.g. <code>@recovery</code>), or <code>!</code> to search <strong>custom fields</strong> &mdash; both labels and values (e.g. <code>!PIN</code>). The count line shows which scope is active. Tag, note, and custom-field search read the decrypted payloads cached in memory, so they cover every entry once both passwords are entered and all names have been revealed.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:6px"><strong>Group A&ndash;Z.</strong> The entry grid is always sorted alphabetically; the <strong>Group A&ndash;Z</strong> toggle (beside the entry count, once the vault is unlocked) adds sticky first-letter headers above each run of entries &mdash; names not starting with a letter are bucketed under <code>#</code>. The preference is remembered per vault in your browser (on by default).</p>
        <p style="margin-top:6px"><strong>Favorites.</strong> Click the &#x2606; star in the display panel (or press <kbd>F</kbd>) to favorite the open entry. Favorites pin to the top of the grid &mdash; under a <strong>&#x2605; Favorites</strong> header when grouping is on &mdash; and show a &#x2605; on their button. The set is remembered per vault in your browser as a <strong>one-way hash of each name</strong> (never the name itself), so it survives edits and reveals nothing if local storage is read.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:6px"><strong>Custom fields.</strong> Entries can carry any number of extra labelled values (added with <strong>&#x2795;&#xFE0E; Add field</strong> in the form), each optionally marked <em>secret</em> to mask it in the panel. Like tags, they live inside the encrypted payload and are as protected as every other field.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:6px"><strong>Secure notes.</strong> The <strong>Type</strong> toggle at the top of the entry form switches between a <strong>Login</strong> (URL, username, password, 2FA) and a <strong>Secure Note</strong> &mdash; just a name plus notes, tags, and custom fields, with the credential rows and password generator hidden. The type travels inside the encrypted payload like tags, so it adds no new record format; note entries show a &#x1F4DD;&#xFE0E; badge on their button and are skipped by Audit (there is no password to check).</p>
        <p style="margin-top:6px"><strong>Multi-select.</strong> The &#x2611;&#xFE0E; <strong>Select</strong> control beside Group A&ndash;Z turns the grid into a checklist &mdash; click entries to toggle their selection, then use the bar that appears at the bottom to <strong>Favorite</strong>, <strong>Tag&hellip;</strong>, or <strong>Delete</strong> all of them in one save. Bulk delete can&rsquo;t empty the vault (leave at least one entry off, or delete the last one individually) and offers an <strong>Undo</strong> for a few seconds afterward; bulk tag merges the typed tags into each selected entry without touching its other fields.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="about-section" id="about-vault-signing">
        <h3>&#x270D;&#xFE0E; Vault Signing</h3>
        <p>Every save automatically signs the entire record set with a <strong>keyed HMAC</strong> that only a password holder can produce. The server stores the signature opaquely — it has no access to your passwords and can never forge or verify it. On every unlock, the browser recomputes the signature over the records it received and compares. Any discrepancy — an entry tampered with, silently deleted, or corrupted — is shown as a red failure badge rather than passing unnoticed.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:8px"><strong>Signature construction</strong></p>
        <table class="cr-table" style="margin-top:4px">
          <tr><td class="cr-name">Vault key</td><td class="cr-desc">HKDF-SHA-256 over <code>Argon2id(pw1, salt1) ‖ Argon2id(pw2, salt2)</code>, info <code>v6|manifest|hmac</code> — independent of every per-record key</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="cr-name">Signed data</td><td class="cr-desc">Header fields (format, salts, revision, timestamp, kdf cost) + every record joined in canonical server order</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="cr-name">Algorithm</td><td class="cr-desc">HMAC-SHA-256 via WebCrypto</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="cr-name">Revision</td><td class="cr-desc">Monotonic counter incremented on every sign; each device remembers the highest revision it has seen, so a validly signed but older copy of the vault raises a <strong>rollback</strong> alert</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="cr-name">KDF binding</td><td class="cr-desc">The active Argon2id cost (<code>a2id|m|t|p</code>) is signed alongside the record set, so a server that swaps in a weaker cost is caught by comparing the manifest's declared cost against the served one, rather than merely making every record fail to decrypt</td></tr>
        </table>
        <p style="margin-top:8px"><strong>Manifest format</strong> (stored server-side, embedded in the served page):</p>
        <pre style="font-size:11px;margin-top:4px;padding:8px;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:var(--r);overflow-x:auto">vm2 | salt1 | salt2 | revision | timestamp | kdf | hmac   (current)
vm1 | salt1 | salt2 | revision | timestamp | hmac          (legacy, still verified; upgraded to vm2 on next write)</pre>
        <p style="margin-top:8px"><strong>Limits:</strong> this is tamper <em>detection</em> for the data file. It guards against corruption, botched restores, and manual-edit mistakes. It cannot defend against an adversary who can also rewrite the served JavaScript — no in-band scheme can.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="about-section">
        <h3>&#x1F5C2;&#xFE0E; Record Formats</h3>
        <p><strong>Record format (v6)&thinsp;—&thinsp;doubly-encrypted name:</strong></p>
        <p><code>encName | v6 | recSalt1 | recSalt2 | nameNonce1 | nameNonce2 | iv1 | nonce2 | nonce3 | nonce4 | ciphertext</code></p>
        <p style="margin-top:4px"><code>recSalt1</code> and <code>recSalt2</code> are the two Argon2id salts (one per master password); every per-cipher key is HKDF-derived from the resulting master keys, so the record carries only nonces, not per-layer salts. The entry name is double-encrypted: <code>AES-256-GCM</code> (password&nbsp;1) as the inner layer, wrapped by <code>ChaCha20-Poly1305</code> (password&nbsp;2) as the outer layer. Both passwords are required to reveal the name. Entry buttons are hidden on page load; they appear automatically when both key fields contain a value and the secondary key field loses focus.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:4px">Records are stored in a flat file, one per line, sorted by the raw bytes of the full encrypted record &mdash; since the name itself is encrypted, that is <em>not</em> alphabetical by name; it just keeps the file in a stable, deterministic order. The entry grid you see is re-sorted alphabetically in the browser, by the real name, after it is decrypted. Each button also carries its line index as an informational 12th field, but it is never used to identify a record &mdash; delete and edit always reference the full record string instead, so a concurrent change elsewhere can't make an operation land on the wrong line.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:4px">The encrypted payload (<code>ciphertext</code>) is a JSON object holding the URL, username, password, 2FA token, notes, tags, <strong>custom fields</strong>, <strong>password history</strong>, <strong>creation</strong> / <strong>password-modified</strong> timestamps, an optional <strong>entry type</strong> (<code>login</code> or secure <strong>note</strong>), and an optional <strong>passkey</strong> (WebAuthn credential). These all live inside the same encrypted blob &mdash; the record format itself is unchanged, so every one of them is as protected as the password.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="about-section">
        <h3>&#x1F511;&#xFE0E; Key Derivation</h3>
        <p>Each record derives <strong>two 256-bit master keys</strong> with memory-hard <code>Argon2id</code> (default <strong>m&nbsp;=&nbsp;128&nbsp;MiB, t&nbsp;=&nbsp;3, p&nbsp;=&nbsp;1</strong>, adjustable per vault in <em>Change KDF Parameters</em>) &mdash; one per master password, each over a <strong>32-byte random salt</strong> generated fresh for every save. <code>HKDF-SHA-256</code> then expands each master key into the independent per-cipher subkeys (distinct <code>info</code> labels), so the expensive memory-hard step runs only <strong>twice per record</strong> while every layer still gets its own key.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:6px">The memory-hard step is what makes an offline brute-force attack infeasible: every single password guess costs a full memory pass (128&nbsp;MiB at the default cost) plus significant CPU time, so a GPU farm that tests billions of fast hashes per second is reduced to a few thousand Argon2id guesses per second per card. Combined with a strong, unique second password, this raises the cost of guessing the vault open to be computationally prohibitive.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:6px">Revealing many entry names at once would otherwise serialise the memory-hard hashes on the main thread (the WASM runs synchronously behind an internal mutex). To keep the UI responsive, each derivation is dispatched to a pool of dedicated <strong>Web Workers</strong> &mdash; one WebAssembly instance each &mdash; so peak memory stays bounded at pool&nbsp;&times;&nbsp;the per-worker heap (128&nbsp;MiB at the default cost). The pool is sized at the <strong>reported CPU core count</strong> &mdash; capped at <strong>24</strong> on desktop and <strong>2</strong> on phones/tablets (less RAM, thermal throttling) &mdash; and further bounded by available device memory so the heaps stay within roughly half of RAM. It is created lazily and torn down on every lock, reclaiming its WASM heaps and dropping residual password bytes. A derivation that fails transiently (memory pressure, a worker crash) is <strong>retried</strong> &mdash; falling back to the main thread between attempts &mdash; so an entry is never silently dropped from the grid; if Workers are unavailable entirely, derivation runs on the main thread.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="about-section">
        <h3>&#x26D3;&#xFE0E; Encryption Pipeline</h3>
        <div class="about-chain">
          <span class="about-chip">Plaintext&nbsp;JSON</span>
          <span>&#x2192;</span>
          <span class="about-chip">Pad&nbsp;to&nbsp;256&nbsp;B</span>
          <span>&#x2192;</span>
          <span class="about-chip">ChaCha20-Poly1305</span>
          <span>&#x2192;</span>
          <span class="about-chip">AES-256-GCM</span>
          <span>&#x2192;</span>
          <span class="about-chip">Twofish-256-CTR</span>
          <span>&#x2192;</span>
          <span class="about-chip">Serpent-256-CTR</span>
          <span>&#x2192;</span>
          <span class="about-chip">Stored&nbsp;hex</span>
        </div>
        <div class="crypto-row">
          <span class="crypto-layer">Layer 1</span>
          <span><code>ChaCha20-Poly1305</code> &mdash; AEAD &mdash; password&nbsp;1, 12-byte nonce</span>
        </div>
        <div class="crypto-row">
          <span class="crypto-layer">Layer 2</span>
          <span><code>AES-256-GCM</code> &mdash; AEAD &mdash; password&nbsp;1, 12-byte IV</span>
        </div>
        <div class="crypto-row">
          <span class="crypto-layer">Layer 3</span>
          <span><code>Twofish-256-CTR</code> &mdash; password&nbsp;2, 16-byte nonce (unauthenticated; relies on inner AEAD layers for integrity)</span>
        </div>
        <div class="crypto-row">
          <span class="crypto-layer">Layer 4</span>
          <span><code>Serpent-256-CTR</code> &mdash; password&nbsp;2, 16-byte nonce (unauthenticated; relies on inner AEAD layers for integrity)</span>
        </div>
        <p style="margin-top:6px">The two inner AEAD layers authenticate the ciphertext. A wrong password or tampered data causes immediate authentication failure before any plaintext is revealed.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:6px"><strong>Both passwords are required.</strong> Layers&nbsp;1&ndash;2 use password&nbsp;1 and layers&nbsp;3&ndash;4 use password&nbsp;2 (the entry name is split the same way &mdash; AES-256-GCM under password&nbsp;1, ChaCha20-Poly1305 under password&nbsp;2), so an attacker who compromises one password alone learns nothing about names or payload.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:6px"><strong>Length-hiding padding.</strong> Before the first layer, the plaintext JSON is padded up to the next <strong>256-byte</strong> boundary with trailing spaces. The stored ciphertext length therefore reveals only which 256-byte bucket an entry falls into &mdash; not the true length of a password or note &mdash; so someone who obtains the raw database cannot tell a short password from a long one. The padding is valid trailing JSON whitespace, so it is discarded automatically on decrypt with no separate un-pad step.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="about-section">
        <h3>&#x1F6E1;&#xFE0E; Cipher Rationale</h3>
        <p>The four layers span <strong>three independent cipher families</strong> from different design lineages. An attacker breaking the stored ciphertext must defeat all four simultaneously &mdash; breaking AES does not weaken ChaCha20, Twofish, or Serpent. Each cipher was chosen to complement the others' failure modes.</p>
        <table class="cr-table">
          <tr>
            <td class="cr-name"><code>ChaCha20-Poly1305</code></td>
            <td class="cr-tag"><span class="cr-aead">AEAD</span></td>
            <td class="cr-desc">Stream cipher designed by Daniel J. Bernstein. No S-box lookups &mdash; constant-time by construction, immune to cache-timing attacks. Adopted in TLS&nbsp;1.3, SSH, and WireGuard as the standard software-path cipher. The Poly1305 tag authenticates the ciphertext; a wrong password or tampered byte causes immediate rejection before any plaintext is produced.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="cr-name"><code>AES-256-GCM</code></td>
            <td class="cr-tag"><span class="cr-aead">AEAD</span></td>
            <td class="cr-desc">The global standard block cipher, NIST-standardised and FIPS&nbsp;140-2 compliant. Hardware-accelerated (AES-NI) on all modern CPUs. The most widely analysed cipher in existence. GCM adds a Galois-field authentication tag, giving full authenticated encryption. Used here as the second AEAD layer so two independent authentication checks must both pass.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="cr-name"><code>Twofish-256-CTR</code></td>
            <td class="cr-tag"><span class="cr-ctr">CTR</span></td>
            <td class="cr-desc">AES finalist (top 5), designed by Bruce Schneier, John Kelsey, Doug Whiting, David Wagner, Chris Hall, and Niels Ferguson. Key-dependent S-boxes and a Feistel structure entirely different from AES&rsquo;s SubBytes/ShiftRows/MixColumns design. Adds a third independent key under password&nbsp;2, so an attacker who knows password&nbsp;1 still cannot read the plaintext.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="cr-name"><code>Serpent-256-CTR</code></td>
            <td class="cr-tag"><span class="cr-ctr">CTR</span></td>
            <td class="cr-desc">AES finalist that came <strong>second</strong> in the panel vote, designed by Ross Anderson, Eli Biham, and Lars Knudsen. Chosen by the designers specifically for its conservative security margin: 32 rounds (vs AES&rsquo;s 14), with a simple bitslice structure that has resisted cryptanalysis for over 25 years. The outermost layer &mdash; any weakness in layers 1&ndash;3 is still protected here.</td>
          </tr>
        </table>
        <p style="margin-top:10px"><strong>Argon2id (PHC winner, 2015)</strong> sits in front of all four ciphers as the memory-hard key-derivation step &mdash; see <em>Key Derivation</em> above for the cost parameters and why they make offline brute-force infeasible.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:6px">The overall result: even if an attacker obtains the raw ciphertext file, they face four independent cipher layers with independent HKDF-derived keys, two independent AEAD authentication checks, and a memory-hard KDF that serialises every guess &mdash; all of which must be overcome before a single password is revealed.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="about-section">
        <h3>&#x23F1;&#xFE0E; TOTP (2FA)</h3>
        <p>The <code>token</code> field accepts a bare Base32 secret &mdash; the common case, defaulting to a 6-digit code on a 30-second window via <code>HMAC-SHA-1</code> (RFC&nbsp;6238) &mdash; or a full <code>otpauth://</code> URI, which can override the digit count, period, and hash algorithm (<code>SHA-1</code> / <code>SHA-256</code> / <code>SHA-512</code>), including <strong>Steam&nbsp;Guard</strong>'s 5-character codes. Either way the token is encrypted with the rest of the entry, and the live code's countdown ring follows whichever period the entry actually uses.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:4px">The <strong>📷 Scan QR</strong> button in the new-entry form uses the browser's <code>BarcodeDetector</code> API to read a TOTP QR code from a photo or image file. A plain default TOTP stores just the secret; a URI with non-default digits/period/algorithm or Steam Guard keeps the full <code>otpauth://</code> URI so those settings survive. The issuer and account name pre-fill the Name and Username fields if they are empty. Requires Chrome&nbsp;83+, Edge&nbsp;83+, or Safari&nbsp;17.4+.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="about-section">
        <h3>&#x1F4CA;&#xFE0E; Password Strength</h3>
        <p>While typing a password in the new-entry form, a six-segment bar and label estimate strength from the character classes present (lowercase, uppercase, digits, symbols) and the password length. Entropy is reported in bits.</p>
        <table style="width:100%;margin-top:6px;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:12px">
          <tr><td style="color:#ff5c5c;font-family:var(--mono);padding:1px 8px 1px 0">Weak</td><td style="color:var(--text-2)">&lt; 40 bits</td></tr>
          <tr><td style="color:#f5a623;font-family:var(--mono);padding:1px 8px 1px 0">Fair</td><td style="color:var(--text-2)">40 – 79 bits</td></tr>
          <tr><td style="color:#3fcf8e;font-family:var(--mono);padding:1px 8px 1px 0">Strong</td><td style="color:var(--text-2)">80 – 119 bits</td></tr>
          <tr><td style="color:#4d8eff;font-family:var(--mono);padding:1px 8px 1px 0">Very Strong</td><td style="color:var(--text-2)">120 – 159 bits</td></tr>
          <tr><td style="color:#a878ff;font-family:var(--mono);padding:1px 8px 1px 0">Exceptional</td><td style="color:var(--text-2)">160 – 199 bits</td></tr>
          <tr><td style="color:#ff5cc8;font-family:var(--mono);padding:1px 8px 1px 0">Paranoid</td><td style="color:var(--text-2)">&ge; 200 bits</td></tr>
        </table>
        <p style="margin-top:8px">The two <strong>master password</strong> fields share a second bar that scores their <em>combined</em> strength &mdash; the sum of both passwords' estimated bits against tiers deliberately compressed below the single-field ones (45 / 80 / 115 / 150 / 185 bits), since the per-password estimate already runs high for dictionary-like input and Argon2id's cost is treated as margin rather than credited bits. If the combined score falls under the lowest tier, the <strong>＋ New</strong> button is disabled and reads <strong>Too Weak</strong> until a stronger pair is entered. The same combined scoring drives the strength bar in <strong>Change Passwords</strong>.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="about-section">
        <h3>&#x1F5C4;&#xFE0E; Key Caching</h3>
        <p>Argon2id master keys are memoised per <code>(password,&nbsp;salt)</code> pair for the duration of the session, so each entry incurs only its two memory-hard derivations once. Clicking an entry after reveal-all reuses the cached master keys (no further Argon2id work). The cache is wiped on auto-lock or manual clear.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="about-section">
        <h3>&#x231B;&#xFE0E; Auto-lock</h3>
        <p>After <strong>5 minutes</strong> of inactivity a 60-second warning appears. If not dismissed, all key fields and cached keys are cleared from memory.</p>
        <p>The <strong>Disable auto-lock</strong> toggle below turns this off; the choice is remembered on this device and stays in effect across reloads and vault locks until you turn it back on. You can also toggle it with the <kbd>U</kbd> key while this panel is open.</p>
        <label class="about-check"><input type="checkbox" id="autolock-disable-toggle"/> Disable auto-lock (not recommended)</label>
      </div>

      <div class="about-section">
        <h3>&#x1F4DA;&#xFE0E; Libraries &amp; Attribution</h3>
        <p>Cipher primitives are third-party open-source, bundled <strong>inline</strong> &mdash; no runtime network requests; Argon2id WASM is embedded as base64. Each is used under its license, upstream notices retained.</p>
